        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-51a22b8927b39e5a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Lorck’s written declaration centrally presents his love as a settled emotional truth. In the courtship letter it also carries some undertaking force by offering a love he portrays as worthy of Constance and seeking a future relationship, though the core clause is more an avowal than an explicit commitment. Its carefully considered, carefully worded form provides no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The performative contribution is moderate: the surrounding letter constitutes a relational overture, but the highlighted declaration itself primarily avows a state rather than promising or committing explicitly.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Nils Lorck writes the declaration to Constance in a letter after several unsuccessful attempts to visit her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lorck frames the declaration as the result of prolonged self-examination and cautiously uses it to initiate explicit courtship. He presents his love as deep, beneficial to Constance, and central to his own happiness, then asks whether he might eventually win her love. Constance understands the letter as a serious romantic overture but immediately declines. The deliberation and epistolary control strongly favor avowal over reflexive discharge; the rejection does not alter the original utterance’s force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“jeg nu har grublet og grundet over mig og Dem i lange Tider, og at jeg ved til Bunden af min Sjæl, at jeg elsker Dem” (“I have pondered deeply over myself and you for a long time, and ... know in the depths of my soul that I love you”).\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional avowal and a deliberately reached conviction; it also weighs against expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck says he is afraid of offending or frightening Constance and does not know how to couch his words.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is cautious and composed rather than a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He declares love “med en Kjærlighed, som De kunde være tjent med” and says that with her lies his happiness in life.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration offers his love as relationally valuable and thus gives moderate support to undertaking or courtship force alongside the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He follows by asking whether, with time and trust, he might win her love.\",\"supports\":\"The letter functions as a serious relational overture, supporting some performative force without turning the core declaration into an explicit promise.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Constance replies immediately that he should not waste time trying to win her love.\",\"supports\":\"Her response confirms that the declaration was received as sincere courtship rather than as quotation, play, or semantically empty language.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the settled avowal, while a moderate P score captures its role in a relational overture. Deliberate written composition supplies no independent E force, and no aspect of the core utterance requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied English translation was used only as an analytical aid; quoted evidence is grounded in the provided source passage.\"}"
        }
